# consumer proof for #141-#150 (this branch, rebased on PR #148)

One host, real installs, every check run as a consumer with nothing
pre-sourced where it matters. Read the command, then the answer: each
section is what was typed and what the machine printed back.

## The host, because exec permissions differ by sandbox

Linux x86_64, glibc, uid 0. Probed, not assumed:

- `/workspace`: exec=yes, 77GB free (the roomy root)
- `/tmp`: exec=no (rc=126: a writable mount refusing execve)
- `/dev/shm`: exec=no
- `$HOME` (`/state/home`): exec=no

So this sandbox is the split shape with the large root on `/workspace`:
HOME cannot run files, `/workspace` can and has room. The planner picks
`/workspace/.sandhome/exec`-style roots on its merits, and `--exec` names
one explicitly. Proxy egress only (`http_proxy=http://169.254.169.1:40295`);
hosts outside the proxy fail, which the npm and go-install sections show.

## Setup: fresh bootstrap with an explicit exec root

```sh
rm -rf /workspace/.consumer && mkdir -p /workspace/.consumer/home /workspace/.consumer/exec
env -i HOME=/workspace/.consumer/home PATH=/usr/bin:/bin TMPDIR=/workspace/.consumer/tmp \
  sh bootstrap.sh --toolset minimal --exec /workspace/.consumer/exec
# failures=0, adopted=jq, memexec=unneeded (the home runs its own files)
```

```sh
sandhome space
# home=/workspace/.consumer/home/.local/share/sandhome home_exec=yes
# exec=/workspace/.consumer/exec exec_reason=split exec_space=ok exec_ceiling=roomy
# payloads=none
sandhome doctor
# ok home_writable=yes, exec_writable=yes, exec_runs=yes, ... toolchain_jq=yes
# doctor_failures=0 (rc=0)
```

## Go: force install, real payload on home, real view on exec, builds

```sh
sandhome install --force go
# Downloaded from: https://go.dev/dl/go1.27.1.linux-amd64.tar.gz with curl
# sha256 matches the value from the published digest
ls -la toolchains/go/go/bin/go views/go/go/bin/go
# 17142188 .../toolchains/go/go/bin/go (payload, home)
# 17142188 .../exec/views/go/go/bin/go (view, exec: a real copy, not a launcher)
. env.sh && go build -o hello . && ./hello
# build rc=0, "hello from sandhome go"
go env GOROOT GOBIN GOCACHE
# .../exec/views/go/go, .../exec/go-bin, .../exec/cache/go-build
sandhome doctor | grep -E 'toolchain_go|split_views'
# ok split_views=ok, ok toolchain_go=yes, doctor_failures=0
```

## Node: browser caches on exec, hook serves a fresh shell

```sh
sandhome install node   # v26.10.0, prefix+cache on the exec root
PUPPETEER_CACHE_DIR=.../exec/puppeteer PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH=.../exec/ms-playwright
mkdir -p /workspace/.consumer/pathbin   # writable PATH dir on the exec volume
sandhome global
# installed the global hook at 1 of 1 directory, verified in a fresh shell
env -i PATH=.../pathbin:/usr/bin:/bin HOME=... sh -c 'command -v node; node --version'
# .../pathbin/node, v26.10.0 (nothing sourced)
```

## #142: installer runs through the hook, next shell needs nothing

```sh
# NOTE: the first attempt installed cowsay from a SOURCED shell, where
# $SANDHOME_EXEC/bin shadows the hook, so no link was made. That path still
# needs one `sandhome global`, as the guide says. Through the hook:
env -i PATH=.../pathbin:... HOME=... SANDHOME_EXEC=... sh -c 'npm install -g cowthink'
ls .../pathbin
# cowsay cowthink go gofmt jq node npm npx sandhome (both CLIs linked on return)
env -i PATH=.../pathbin:/usr/bin:/bin HOME=... sh -c 'command -v cowsay; cowsay "hook works"'
# .../pathbin/cowsay, prints the cow (nothing sourced, no manual global)
```

The npm registry needed the proxy variables passed into `env -i`;
without them npm answers EAI_AGAIN while curl works. That is the sandbox,
not the tree.

## #141: gc keeps a real go-install binary, reclaims the cache

```sh
go install golang.org/x/tools/cmd/stringer@latest   # 8573159 bytes in go-bin
touch -d '2 hours ago' $SANDHOME_EXEC/go-bin/stringer
sandhome gc 0
# removed 0 entries (the CLI survives); a backdated cache blob: removed, 21KB reclaimed
ls $SANDHOME_EXEC/go-bin/stringer && stringer -help
# present, runs, rc=0
sandhome space --largest 8
# views (sandhome), cache (reclaim), npm-global (sandhome), tmp (reclaim),
# .sandhome-lib (sandhome), bin (sandhome)
# tag: reclaim = gc removes this; sandhome = this tree owns it and gc keeps it;
#      yours = you installed or built it, remove it yourself
```

## #147: env -i works straight after setup, no repair

```sh
ls $SANDHOME_EXEC/.sandhome-lib/lib/common.sh   # present
env -i $SANDHOME_EXEC/bin/sandhome version       # sandhome/1, rc=0
```

## #145: pty names a terminal; #144: sanitizers keep their output

```sh
env -u TERM sandhome pty less -F /tmp/f.txt    # prints line1 line2 (was 'unknown')
env -u TERM sandhome pty sh -c 'printf $TERM'  # TERM=xterm-256color
# SANDHOME_PTRACE=no, ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=0 LSAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=0 (from env.sh)
clang -fsanitize=address -g hello.c -o h_asan && ./h_asan
# hello from C: asan, rc=0
env -u ASAN_OPTIONS -u LSAN_OPTIONS ./h_asan
# LeakSanitizer has encountered a fatal error ... does not work under ptrace, rc=1
```

## #146: qemu guests, one message, archive kept

```sh
sandhome install qemuuser --extra aarch64
# toolchain qemuuser: not present; installing ...; Downloaded ... 11.1.1.1 ...
# no "rebuilding without downloading" / "does not run" contradiction
ls toolchains/qemuuser/qu.tar.xz   # kept for the next --extra
sandhome install qemuuser --extra arm   # second guest from the kept bytes
```

A later `--extra aarch64 --extra arm` run re-resolved the tag while codeberg
stalled and hung on the 63MB fetch; the kept archive had already been
deleted on the pin mismatch, which is why the install now records `qu.tag`
beside the archive, reuses the recorded release without the network, and
falls back to it when tag resolution fails. The guest set is per-install by
design (a clean bin each run, so BINS never promises a dropped guest):
name every wanted guest on one command.

